  ],
  "text": "About five hundred cables on the ocean floor carry over ninety five percent of all international data. The word cloud does a lot of work here. There is nothing cloudlike about any of it. Satellites carry a tiny fraction. Almost everything goes through glass on the seabed. Cut one open and the glass threads at the centre are thinner than a hair. Everything around them is armour, and out in deep water there is barely any. Which makes the causes of most outages disappointingly ordinary. Ship anchors. Fishing gear dragged across the bottom. Earthquakes. Undersea landslides. There are ships whose entire job is to find the break, haul the cable up, and splice it. It happens more than a hundred times a year, and you almost never notice. The cloud is a bundle of glass threads lying in the dark, being repaired by people on boats. One of these every day. Follow so you do not miss tomorrow.",
  "voice": "802e3bc2b27e49c2995d23ef70e6ac89",
  "model": "s2.1-pro-free"
}
